{
	title: 'Terminal blocks link to their web card',
	spec: "Every block the terminal draws that has a block id (prompt, text, thinking, tool call, question, command, output, error; a tool result links to its call instead of its own block) shows that id dim at the right edge of its first row, e.g. #35, in the block's own colour; hover and underline are the terminal's. The id is an OSC 8 link to the same block on the web: <webUrl>/<session id>#<block id>, the id task 0z uses, with the hidden ?auth= link code of task e3; the visible text shows no code. A gutter as wide as the id plus one column stays free on every row of the block, as the web keeps a column for its link, and a streaming block's Hal cursor follows its text, not the id. On a terminal narrower than four times the id, the id is left out. A block without an id (a hand-built item, key ~<position>) shows none. tasks/README.md's rule: anything linkable in one client is linkable in the other. Tests: each block's id is at the right edge and links to the address the web resolves to that block; the visible rows show no code; a tool result links to the recorded call id and a block without an id shows none; the streaming cursor sits before the id.",
	status: 'done',
	needs: ['0z', 'e3'],
	foldInto: ['0z'],
	notes: [
		"Hal's terminal draws no header rows, ref or time (items start with a marker: '> ', '▸ ', '? ', 'error:'), so the link sits on what stands in for the header: a tool call's whole row (the terminal shows a glimpse, the card all of it), else the leading marker token. No visible ref was added: it would steal columns from wrapped prompt text. itemView.linked does it after itemLines; frame.itemRows takes the session id (transcript.meta.id) and caches per session and key.",
		'ansi.webUrl puts ?auth= before a #fragment: appended after it, the code would land in the hash and the page would get neither the code nor the block id.',
		'The block-id rule (digits, optional .<i>; `~` keys are not linkable) lives in common transcript.href, shared by web target.ts and the terminal. The frame test resolves terminal links with web target.parse (tests may cross layers).',
		'Tool results show an inline #<call block id> hyperlink to the call, which the web folds with its result in the same card. A background bash message also links the #<call block id> in its header; its own message id still links to itself. Text, thinking and command output retain their own links.'
	]
}
